WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:03.846
In part one

2
00:00:03.924 --> 00:00:06.840
the signs of the Fiedler vector split

3
00:00:06.919 --> 00:00:09.126
a graph into its two groups.

4
00:00:09.126 --> 00:00:11.096
Now add a ninth student

5
00:00:11.096 --> 00:00:13.460
who knows only student eight.

6
00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:16.743
There are now two ways to cut a single edge

7
00:00:16.743 --> 00:00:19.010
the bridge between four and five

8
00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:21.143
or the edge from eight to nine

9
00:00:21.143 --> 00:00:23.010
which cuts off one student.

10
00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:24.894
A natural rule

11
00:00:24.981 --> 00:00:27.590
the best split is the one that

12
00:00:27.677 --> 00:00:29.590
cuts the fewest edges.

13
00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:33.127
But here both cuts remove one edge

14
00:00:33.218 --> 00:00:36.495
and in larger graphs this rule loves

15
00:00:36.586 --> 00:00:39.500
to cut off a single lonely node.

16
00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:42.962
A better measure is the ratio cut

17
00:00:42.962 --> 00:00:44.335
the cut edges

18
00:00:44.335 --> 00:00:48.454
times one over the size of each side, added.

19
00:00:48.545 --> 00:00:51.291
Cutting off student nine costs

20
00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:55.410
one plus one eighth: one point one two five.

21
00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.928
Splitting at the bridge costs

22
00:00:58.003 --> 00:00:59.723
a quarter plus a fifth

23
00:00:59.723 --> 00:01:01.367
zero point four five.

24
00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:03.760
Balanced sides make it cheaper.

25
00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:06.802
Minimising x transpose L x

26
00:01:06.802 --> 00:01:10.540
with x perpendicular to the ones vector

27
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:12.728
is a relaxed ratio cut

28
00:01:12.728 --> 00:01:16.740
real numbers instead of plus and minus one.

29
00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.373
So the Fiedler vector does not

30
00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.352
fall for the lonely node.

31
00:01:21.352 --> 00:01:24.778
Student nine gets minus zero point four six

32
00:01:24.778 --> 00:01:27.061
on the side of five to eight

33
00:01:27.061 --> 00:01:30.410
and the sign change is still at the bridge.

34
00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:31.817
Now data.

35
00:01:31.908 --> 00:01:34.113
Thirty made-up points on

36
00:01:34.204 --> 00:01:36.133
a ring of radius one

37
00:01:36.225 --> 00:01:38.980
and ninety on a ring of radius

38
00:01:39.072 --> 00:01:42.470
three around it, with a little noise.

39
00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.269
Data does not come as a graph

40
00:01:45.269 --> 00:01:46.657
so we build one.

41
00:01:46.738 --> 00:01:49.024
Two points get a weight near

42
00:01:49.106 --> 00:01:50.902
one if they are close

43
00:01:50.902 --> 00:01:53.187
and near zero if far apart.

44
00:01:53.187 --> 00:01:54.983
Sigma sets the width.

45
00:01:54.983 --> 00:01:57.922
Then form the Laplacian, D minus W

46
00:01:57.922 --> 00:01:58.820
as before.

47
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.832
First try k means.

48
00:02:00.832 --> 00:02:02.899
It looks for two centres

49
00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:06.080
and gives each point to the nearer one.

50
00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.788
But both rings have the same centre.

51
00:02:08.853 --> 00:02:10.622
Two centres split the plane

52
00:02:10.687 --> 00:02:11.932
by a straight line

53
00:02:11.997 --> 00:02:14.682
and k means agrees with the rings on only

54
00:02:14.748 --> 00:02:16.844
fifty two percent of the points

55
00:02:16.844 --> 00:02:18.350
no better than a coin.

56
00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.250
Now take the similarity graph

57
00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:22.930
with sigma one half.

58
00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:25.890
Will the signs of its Fiedler vector

59
00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:27.810
separate the two rings?

60
00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.170
Pause and guess.

61
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:34.187
Yes, perfectly

62
00:02:34.187 --> 00:02:37.730
every point lands with its own ring.

63
00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:41.178
The Laplacian looks at connections

64
00:02:41.178 --> 00:02:42.710
not at centres.

65
00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.943
The eigenvalues show why.

66
00:02:44.943 --> 00:02:47.730
Lambda two, about seven thousandths

67
00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.764
is far below lambda three

68
00:02:49.764 --> 00:02:51.496
about four hundredths

69
00:02:51.496 --> 00:02:54.660
the rings are two almost separate pieces.

70
00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.343
Now widen the weights to

71
00:02:57.431 --> 00:02:59.446
sigma zero point eight.

72
00:02:59.533 --> 00:03:02.250
Points on different rings still

73
00:03:02.337 --> 00:03:04.440
count as fairly similar.

74
00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:07.720
Lambda two and lambda three are close

75
00:03:07.797 --> 00:03:09.724
zero point four eight and

76
00:03:09.801 --> 00:03:11.497
zero point five three.

77
00:03:11.497 --> 00:03:12.808
There is no gap

78
00:03:12.885 --> 00:03:15.506
and the sign split agrees with the

79
00:03:15.583 --> 00:03:18.127
rings on only fifty four percent.

80
00:03:18.204 --> 00:03:20.980
So look at the eigenvalue gap before

81
00:03:21.057 --> 00:03:22.830
you trust the clusters.

82
00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:27.037
For k groups, take the eigenvectors of the

83
00:03:27.124 --> 00:03:29.110
k smallest eigenvalues.

84
00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:32.650
They give every point k new coordinates

85
00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:36.450
and k means on those coordinates now works.

86
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.656
Libraries usually use the

87
00:03:38.731 --> 00:03:40.290
normalised Laplacian

88
00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:43.408
which balances groups of different sizes.

89
00:03:43.483 --> 00:03:46.007
The same tools find communities in

90
00:03:46.081 --> 00:03:48.160
networks and segment images.

91
00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:50.326
Working with AI.

92
00:03:50.326 --> 00:03:52.704
Asked for three groups

93
00:03:52.704 --> 00:03:56.767
an assistant calls numpy's eig function

94
00:03:56.767 --> 00:03:59.740
and uses columns one and two.

95
00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.600
But eig returns the eigenvalues

96
00:04:02.681 --> 00:04:04.542
in no particular order.

97
00:04:04.542 --> 00:04:06.647
Column one is lambda two

98
00:04:06.647 --> 00:04:09.804
but column two belongs to the largest

99
00:04:09.804 --> 00:04:12.961
four point nine: the roughest pattern.

100
00:04:12.961 --> 00:04:15.713
For a symmetric matrix, use eigh

101
00:04:15.713 --> 00:04:17.170
which sorts them.

102
00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:18.554
To sum up.

103
00:04:18.634 --> 00:04:20.644
The Fiedler vector solves

104
00:04:20.724 --> 00:04:22.332
a relaxed ratio cut

105
00:04:22.332 --> 00:04:24.662
so it finds balanced splits

106
00:04:24.662 --> 00:04:26.350
not just small cuts.

107
00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:29.350
For data, build a similarity graph

108
00:04:29.426 --> 00:04:31.924
and use the smallest eigenvectors

109
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.953
of its Laplacian: groups by connection

110
00:04:34.953 --> 00:04:36.089
not by centre.

111
00:04:36.089 --> 00:04:39.420
Trust them when the eigenvalues show a gap.

112
00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:40.772
Now you try

113
00:04:40.772 --> 00:04:44.361
find the eigenvalues of the path one, two

114
00:04:44.361 --> 00:04:44.945
three

115
00:04:44.945 --> 00:04:48.450
and say how its Fiedler vector splits it.

116
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.258
Next elective: flows through networks

117
00:04:52.349 --> 00:04:54.714
and games where one side's

118
00:04:54.805 --> 00:04:57.080
gain is the other's loss.
